1
00:00:13,620 --> 00:00:18,680
Check your system under pressure for leaks prior to using the contents of the gas cylinder.

2
00:00:19,120 --> 00:00:24,560
Confirm the integrity of the connection by using a bubble test or electronic leak detection instrument.

3
00:00:25,080 --> 00:00:27,260
Never use a flame to check for leaks.

4
00:00:28,320 --> 00:00:35,480
Refer to your laboratory SOP to make sure your method and procedure is compatible with the gas being tested.

5
00:00:36,380 --> 00:00:39,980
A bubble test is performed with SNOOP or a

6
00:00:40,380 --> 00:00:46,100
compatible commercial leak test solution or mild soap solution in a wash bottle.

7
00:00:46,900 --> 00:00:51,740
Apply the solution to the inlet valve and outlet flow valve connections.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:00:56,100
Any active bubbles indicate a leak in your system.

9
00:00:56,900 --> 00:01:01,060
Please note the soap and water may be corrosive to metal fittings.

10
00:01:02,620 --> 00:01:07,060
Electronic leak detection instruments must be maintained and calibrated to ensure accuracy.

11
00:01:08,060 --> 00:01:13,380
Read, understand, and follow the meter's operation manual before use.

12
00:01:13,860 --> 00:01:20,260
If leaks are discovered in your system, depressurize, tighten, and then recheck the connections.

13
00:01:32,720 --> 00:01:38,080
If a leak continues, notify your laboratory supervisor as soon as possible.

14
00:01:39,080 --> 00:01:43,680
Maintain a system inspection schedule to log the cylinder leak tests.

15
00:01:43,680 --> 00:01:48,960
Refer to your laboratory SOP for how to keep and maintain this log.
